WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:04.750
_ This is super exciting
because it's not the academic

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:05.740
problem of NLP,

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:08.100
of Natural Language Processing,
understanding dialogue.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.150
This is like the real world.
- [Guest] This is real world.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:13.670
- The stakes are high in
the sense that customers

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:16.394
get frustrated quickly,
people get frustrated quickly.

00:00:16.394 --> 00:00:18.179
So you have to get it right.

00:00:18.179 --> 00:00:19.810
You have to get that interaction right.

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:21.953
So it's, I love it.

00:00:21.953 --> 00:00:24.190
So from that perspective,

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.920
what are the challenges today?

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:30.050
What are the problems that
really need to be solved

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:30.883
in the next few years.

00:00:30.883 --> 00:00:32.950
- I think first and foremost,

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:35.930
as I mentioned that

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:38.090
to get the basics right are still true.

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:42.030
Basically, even the one shot request,

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:43.850
which we think of as transaction request

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:46.700
needs to work magically,
no question about that.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:48.590
If it doesn't turn your light on and off,

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:50.210
you'll be super frustrated.

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.240
Even if I can complete
the night out for you and

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.750
not do that, that is unacceptable
as a customer, right?

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:58.792
So that, you have to get the
foundational understanding

00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:00.440
going where you will.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.760
The second aspect when I
said more conversational,

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.140
is as you imagine, is
more about reasoning.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:09.350
It is really about figuring
out what the latent goal is

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:13.520
of the customer, based on what
I have the information now,

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:14.740
and the history.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.380
What's the next best thing to do?

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:19.310
So that's a complete
reasoning and decision making

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:21.537
problem just like your self-driving car,

00:01:21.537 --> 00:01:23.670
but the goal is still more finite.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.027
Here, it evolves.

00:01:25.027 --> 00:01:27.850
Environment is super hard in self-driving.

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:31.240
And the cost of a mistake is huge.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:33.900
Here, but there are
certain similarities but,

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:37.660
if you think about how many
decisions Alexa is making

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:39.280
or evaluating at any given time,

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.143
it's a huge hypothesis space.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:44.780
We're only talked about so far,

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:47.570
about what I think of
reactive decision in terms of

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:50.940
you ask for something and
Alexa is reacting to it.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:52.790
If you bring the proactive part,

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:55.070
which is Alexa having hunches,

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:59.440
say any given instance,
Danny, it's really a decision

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.230
at any given point based
on the information.

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:05.150
Alexa has to determine what's
the best thing it needs to do.

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:08.290
So these are the ultimate
A.I. problem with decisions

00:02:08.290 --> 00:02:10.093
based on the information you have.

